= Wave 0 — The First Flame

_Your first game of Civilization IV: Beyond the Sword — how to set it up, what you are looking at, and what to do. All vanilla BTS 3.19._

== The Story

#storybox[
  The night was cold and the tribe was small. When the first spark caught in the dry grass, the circle of faces fell silent — here was light that did not flee and warmth that did not die. They fed it with sticks and swore never to let it go out. Where the river bent and the hills stood guard, they raised their first huts around the fire. The smoke climbed over a settlement without a name, but it would earn one. Every city you will ever build begins the same way: with one flame, held against the dark.
]

== Game Setup

For your first game, choose *Custom Game* from the single-player menu and set:

- *Difficulty: Settler* — the easiest level: slow rivals, cheap mistakes. Pick *Chieftain* for more pushback.
- *Map: Continents, size Small* — a few landmasses and a handful of rivals.
- *Speed: Normal* — the default pace, so this guide matches your screen.
- Leave every other option at its default; if wandering barbarians feel like too much, switch them *Off* for the first game.

#info[
  Choose a leader with the *Financial* trait — for example *Roosevelt* of America or *Isabella* of Spain. Financial earns extra gold from rich tiles, which quietly smooths over beginner mistakes.
]

== What Kind of Game Is This

Civilization is a turn-based empire-building game. You start in 4000 BC with one settlement and one exploring unit, and play until someone wins. On your turn you decide what each city builds, where units move, what your people research, and how you treat the civilizations you meet. Then you press end turn and the world moves: rivals act, barbarians stir, events happen — and control returns to you. Nothing proceeds without your say-so, and that is the appeal: your empire is a machine you assemble one decision at a time. Several paths lead to victory, from conquest to culture to science.

== The Screen at a Glance

The map fills the screen, with your units standing on tiles. Around it:

- *The main map* — tiles show terrain, resources, and improvements. Click a unit to select it; right-click a tile to move it there.
- *The city list* — on the left, each city with its build and growth. Click a city to open its screen.
- *The top bar* — four sliders: research (beakers), gold, culture, and espionage, plus your treasury and the date.
- *The minimap* — a tiny view of the world; click it to jump the camera.
- *The end-turn button* — press it when your turn is done.

#tip[
  Before you press end turn, glance at the city list — a finished build or a full food box means a decision is waiting. A few seconds of checking keeps nothing idle.
]

== Your First City

Your starting settler founds your capital. The two numbers that matter in the city screen are *food* and *production* (hammers). Food grows the population: when the food box fills, the city gains a citizen, who works a tile and adds its yields. Production builds things, one hammer at a time.

Your first build should be a *warrior* if you have no defender yet, or a *work boat* if the capital is on the coast. Then build a *worker* — improvements are the engine of the early game — and after that a *settler* for your second city. Two rules: never let the city sit idle, and never send your only defender far from home.

== Workers and Improvements

Workers build *improvements* on the tiles your citizens work:

- *Farm* — extra food, best on grassland and flood plains beside fresh water.
- *Mine* — extra production. Best on hills.
- *Cottage* — extra commerce (gold and research). It grows over time into a hamlet, village, and town, so build early and leave it alone.
- *Road* — faster movement and connections to your capital; connect your first resource as soon as you can.

Build what the land offers: food-rich ground wants farms and cottages, hill country wants mines.

== Research

Research is how your empire advances. You pick one technology at a time on the *tech tree*, and your research slider turns commerce into *beakers*. Keep the slider high — 90% or 100% when the treasury allows.

A simple first path: *Agriculture* or *Mining*, whichever the land needs; then *Pottery* for granaries and cottages; then *Writing* for libraries. After that, *Animal Husbandry* if livestock or horses are near, or *Bronze Working* if you have copper or forests to clear. One goal at a time.

#tip[
  If the treasury runs low, dip the science slider for a few turns, then push it back up. Accept that research takes time.
]

== Units and Combat Basics

Every unit has a *strength* value, and combat compares attacker against defender with bonuses applied. Terrain matters: defending on a hill, in a forest, or across a river grants large bonuses, so fight from good positions. Units that survive combat earn experience and *promotions* — pick ones that suit the job, like extra strength for attackers or city defense for garrison troops. Keep a few strong units in every city; an empty city is a tempting target. And never attack with outdated units: when you can build stronger, upgrade or replace.

#warn[
  When the game warns that enemy units are near a city, take it seriously: hunker down, build defenders, and fight on your own ground.
]

== Happiness and Health

Every city has a *happiness* limit and a *health* limit. Unhappy citizens refuse to work, and unhealthy ones can die — either way you lose output. The fixes: *luxury resources* like wine, silk, or gems improve happiness once connected; temples and colosseums add happiness; granaries and aqueducts improve health. And if a build cannot wait, the *whip* — the Slavery civic, unlocked by Bronze Working — converts population into production, at the cost of some grumbling.

== Economy

The economy is what your cities produce in *commerce*, the third yield after food and production. Commerce flows through the top-bar sliders and becomes beakers (research) and gold (your treasury); the *science slider* sets the split. Cottages are your early commerce engine; *specialists* — citizens assigned jobs like scientist or priest — matter later, once buildings support them. The short version: grow food, work cottages, keep the science slider high.

== Culture and Borders

Borders are set by *culture*. Cities produce it from buildings — a monument is the cheapest early source — and from wonders. As culture accumulates, a city's borders grow through four levels, claiming more tiles and resources. Culture also presses on neighbors: a border city with lots of it can flip tiles, even foreign cities, toward you. Build a monument early in every new city.

== Religion

Religions are *founded* by researching their tech first: *Buddhism* from Meditation, *Hinduism* from Polytheism, *Judaism* from Monotheism, and later Confucianism, Taoism, Christianity, and Islam. The founding city becomes the religion's *holy city*. Religions spread between cities, your own and foreign ones, and each city with a faith can build its temple and missionaries. Adopting a *state religion* makes cities with that faith happier and lets you spread it, at the price of annoying neighbors who follow other gods. You never must found one; if one spreads to you, enjoy it.

== Diplomacy

When you meet another civilization, the diplomacy screen opens a conversation: trade resources and technologies, sign open borders, declare war or make peace, and — late in the game — *defensive pacts*, where two nations pledge to defend each other. Beginner basics: trade away surplus resources — an extra one earns nothing sitting idle — and keep at least one friendly neighbor. The other leaders have long memories.

== Victory Types

- *Conquest* — capture every rival's original capital.
- *Domination* — own a large majority of the world's land and population.
- *Cultural* — raise three cities to legendary culture (50,000 culture each).
- *Space Race* — build the spaceship to Alpha Centauri first.
- *Diplomatic* — win a vote in the United Nations.
- *Time* — if nobody wins by 2050, the highest score takes it.

== Common Beginner Mistakes

- Letting cities sit idle — always have a build queued, even a cheap one.
- Sending your only defender to explore — garrison the city; explore with a spare unit.
- Building improvements the land does not want — build what the terrain offers.
- Ignoring happiness and health until cities stop growing — check the city screen and fix the cause.
- Attacking with outdated units — build or upgrade something stronger first.
- Running the treasury dry — keep a small gold buffer; dip the science slider when needed.
- Trading away resources you need — only trade your surplus.

